-- title:
--   Kernel of degree p^h is killed by p^h
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a commutative Noetherian ring in which the image of $p$ is nilpotent. Let $X$ and $Y$ be formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules over $B$, that is, each consists of a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F$ over $B$ together with a pair of power series $\varpi$ and an action $a \mapsto \mathrm{act}(a)$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ by pairs of power series in two variables, all of them endomorphisms of $F$, with $\mathrm{act}(1)$ the identity, $\mathrm{act}(ab) = \mathrm{act}(a) \circ \mathrm{act}(b)$, $\mathrm{act}(a+b)$ the $F$-sum of $\mathrm{act}(a)$ and $\mathrm{act}(b)$, $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(p)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt vector Frobenius $\sigma$. Let $\rho = (\rho_1,\rho_2)$ be a pair of power series in $B[[X_1,X_2]]$ which is a homomorphism of formal group laws from $X.F$ to $Y.F$ and satisfies $\rho \circ X.\mathrm{act}(a) = Y.\mathrm{act}(a) \circ \rho$ for all $a \in \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ and $\rho \circ X.\varpi = Y.\varpi \circ \rho$. Let $h$ be a natural number and assume that $\rho$ has kernel of degree $p^h$: the quotient $B[[X_1,X_2]]/(\rho_1,\rho_2)$ is a finite projective $B$-module and, for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f : B \to \kappa$, the quotient of $\kappa[[X_1,X_2]]$ by the ideal generated by the coefficientwise images of $\rho_1,\rho_2$ has $\kappa$-dimension $p^h$. Then both components of $X.\mathrm{act}(p^h)$, where $p^h$ is the $h$-th power of the image of $p$ in $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$, lie in the ideal $(\rho_1,\rho_2)$ of $B[[X_1,X_2]]$.
--
--   This is Deligne's theorem that a finite locally free commutative group scheme of order $n$ is annihilated by $n$, in the form needed for formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules: the kernel of $\rho$, cut out by the Hopf ideal $(\rho_1,\rho_2)$, has order $p^h$, so multiplication by $p^h$ on $X$ vanishes on it. It feeds the statement that $\mathrm{act}(p^h)$ additionally factors through $\rho$, used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development.
